WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.897
Environmental conditions are core flight-risk factors because small unmanned aircraft operate with limited power, mass, sensing, and communication margins.

00:00:08.897 --> 00:00:17.359
Conditions that seem minor on the ground can affect control, endurance, navigation, and image quality in the air.

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:23.652
Weather includes wind, gusts, precipitation, temperature, humidity, visibility, and rapidly changing local effects.

00:00:23.652 --> 00:00:32.982
Terrain, buildings, trees, water, and surface heating can shape airflow and create conditions that a distant forecast does not describe.

00:00:32.982 --> 00:00:37.105
Lighting affects both pilot awareness and sensor performance.

00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:45.134
Glare, deep shadow, low light, haze, and a bright horizon can hide obstacles or produce unusable imagery.

00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:52.294
A technically completed flight may still fail if the data cannot answer the mission question.

00:00:52.294 --> 00:00:56.417
Temperature affects batteries, electronics, motors, operators, and payloads.

00:00:56.417 --> 00:01:00.540
Obstacles and terrain affect clearance and recovery options.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:07.267
Radio interference, metal structures, and limited sky view can reduce command-link or navigation confidence.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.653
Environmental risk is dynamic.

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:18.550
A preflight forecast is a starting point, while site observations and in-flight trends determine whether the plan remains valid.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:24.843
Operators need decision thresholds and enough margin to return before conditions become critical.

00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:28.531
The goal is safe, lawful, conservative operation.

00:01:28.531 --> 00:01:33.522
Good pilots do not prove capability by forcing a mission.

00:01:33.522 --> 00:01:43.721
They recognize reduced margins early, communicate clearly, and delay, modify, return, land, or cancel when the environment no longer supports the operation.
